---
// PALETTE TEST — three tiny marks stacked in the bottom-right corner that
// recolour the site. The accent, the background, the three surface steps, both
// text colours and the selection colour all move together; the palettes are in
// src/styles/palettes.css.
//
//   ▫  off    clear, back to the site's own palette
//   ▪  light  a light palette, drawn at random
//   ▪  dark   a dark palette, drawn at random
//
// Off comes FIRST because it is the exit, and because it is the one that comes
// and goes: it is displayed only while a palette is applied — an off switch for
// something already off is a mark that does nothing. It is CSS that hides it
// (`:root[data-palette]` below), not script, so it is never briefly visible on a
// page that loads with no palette.
//
// It also ARRIVES rather than appearing. In the corner it rises half a slot into
// place; in the footer row it comes out from behind the pair travelling left
// while they travel right, and the three settle centred. Both on the site's own
// critically damped spring, both reversed on the way out. The rules and the
// reasoning are at the foot of this file.
//
// TWO PLACEMENTS, ONE COMPONENT. Above 1024px — the width at which the nav
// stops collapsing into a hamburger, so the same line the rest of the chrome
// changes on — the marks are a fixed vertical stack in the bottom-right corner,
// on top of the page and reachable without scrolling. Below it they leave the
// corner entirely and become a centred row at the FOOT of the page, in normal
// flow after <main>: on a phone a floating control sits over the writing on a
// screen that has no room to spare, and the reader has to hit a 35px target
// they did not ask for. In the footer it is found by whoever goes looking, which
// is the right audience for a colour toy, and everything about it — the hit
// area, the marks, the spacing — is a quarter larger for a thumb.
//
// THE OFF MARK IS THE ONE COLOUR ON SCREEN, not a preview: an outline in
// var(--accent), the accent it is about to take away, with nothing inside it.
// The two filled marks below wear the accent of the palette THEY will apply —
// see src/lib/palette.client.ts, which sets --palette-dot-light/-dark and holds
// the rest of the reasoning.
//
// Rendered on every route, INCLUDING the home page — but on the home page only
// for a visitor who has already pressed one of these somewhere else. That page
// is one composed screen with a single idea on it, and three more things in the
// corner are exactly what it is built not to have; someone arriving for the
// first time meets it in the site's own colours. Someone who went and picked a
// palette keeps it here, controls and all. The `home` prop below carries the
// difference, and Base.astro is the only file that mounts this.
//
// THE WHOLE FEATURE IS THIS FILE, palettes.css, src/lib/palette*.ts, and the
// marked lines in Base.astro. The stylesheet is imported HERE rather than in
// Base or in tokens.css, so deleting this component takes the CSS with it.
import "../styles/palettes.css";

interface Props {
  /**
   * Rendered on the home page, where the marks are conditional.
   *
   * They are in the markup on every route, but on the home page they are hidden
   * until `data-palette-used` says this visitor has pressed one of them
   * somewhere else — see PALETTE_USED_KEY in src/lib/palette.ts for why that is a
   * separate, sticky flag and not just "a palette is applied".
   *
   * At phone widths it also trims the margins: the home page's lock makes <main>
   * yield exactly the height the row needs, so the row sits at the bottom of that
   * one screen with only the gap below it stated. See the stylesheet — being in
   * flow there is what keeps the marks clear of a phone's browser chrome.
   */
  home?: boolean;
}
const { home = false } = Astro.props;
---

{
  /* Buttons, not divs with handlers: these are controls, so they need to be tab
    stops, respond to Enter and Space, and announce themselves. All three
    accessible names are rewritten by palette.client.ts on every change, because
    the name is where the current palette is stated for anyone who cannot see the
    colour. The names here are the fallback for the moment before the script runs
    — and for the case where it never does, in which case the whole corner is
    removed rather than left inert.

    DOM order is top-to-bottom on screen, so tab order and reading order are the
    same thing. */
}
<div class:list={["palette-corner", home && "palette-corner--home"]} data-palette-corner>
  <button
    type="button"
    class="palette-dot palette-dot--off"
    data-palette-clear
    aria-label="Clear the colour palette"></button>
  <button
    type="button"
    class="palette-dot palette-dot--light"
    data-palette-light
    aria-label="Apply a light colour palette"></button>
  <button
    type="button"
    class="palette-dot palette-dot--dark"
    data-palette-dark
    aria-label="Apply a dark colour palette"></button>
</div>

<script>
  import "../lib/palette.client";
</script>

<style>
  /* `position: fixed` puts the stack above the page rather than at the end of
     it, so it is reachable without scrolling to the bottom of a long post.
     z-index 200 clears the content and the header's own layers (chrome.css: 50,
     300) but stays under the mobile menu (400) and the skip link (1000) — a
     colour toy must never sit on top of the navigation or the first tab stop on
     the page.

     IT STANDS IN THE CHROME COLUMN, not at the edge of the window. Offset from
     the right of the viewport, the marks sat on a different vertical line from
     one page to the next: the home page cannot scroll and so has no scrollbar,
     every page that can scroll has one, and a scrollbar narrows the box a fixed
     element measures `right` against — sliding the marks inward by its full
     width. The nav does not follow them, because it is not measured that way:
     `width: var(--site-w); margin-inline: auto` (chrome.css) centres a 94vw
     column in that box, so the same scrollbar moves its right edge by half as
     much. Two elements, two different answers, a visible drift between them.

     So: be the same column. Same width, same `margin-inline: auto`, marks
     pushed to its end — the mechanism chrome.css chose over padding precisely to
     avoid a 100vw/scrollbar mismatch, and the one Carousel.astro already uses to
     land its arrows "on the same right edge as the nav's last button".
     --chrome-w and NOT --site-w: identical at :root, but main.is-centered
     narrows --site-w to the reading measure, and tokens.css keeps --chrome-w for
     exactly this case — something that has to line up with the header. */
  .palette-corner {
    /* The geometry, in one place: a hit area, and the mark drawn inside it. The
       mobile block at the foot of this file re-states these two and nothing
       else — every rule between here and there is written in terms of them, so
       one scale factor moves the lot, the alignment overhang included. */
    --dot-hit: 1.75rem;
    --dot-mark: 0.75rem;
    /* How far the off mark travels, and — on mobile — how far the pair slides to
       make room for it. HALF A SLOT is not a taste decision: three marks centred
       and two marks centred differ by exactly half a slot, so this is the
       distance the pair has to cover, and giving the off mark the same travel is
       what makes the two halves of the move read as one gesture. */
    --dot-shift: calc(var(--dot-hit) / 2);

    /* THE SLOT EACH MARK STANDS IN, counted from its own natural place in the
       stack. The off mark takes the slot immediately above (desktop) or to the
       left of (mobile) whichever square is applied, so:

         light applied   off 0  light 0     ▫ ▪light ▪dark   — nobody moves
         dark applied    off 1  light −1    ▪light ▫ ▪dark   — light is pushed clear

       Two numbers rather than a `flex-direction` reshuffle or a change of
       `order`, because neither of those can be animated: a reordered flex line
       jumps. These feed a `translate`, which is a compositor-friendly property
       and the one thing here that CAN carry the push. */
    --off-place: 0;
    --light-place: 0;
    /* Mobile only: with nothing applied the row is still three slots wide, so
       the pair sits half a slot left of its slots to be centred on its own. */
    --pair-rest: calc(var(--dot-shift) * -1);

    /* The site's spring, the same linear() curve the mobile menu slides on
       (--menu-spring in chrome.css) and the carousel panel rises on
       (--panel-spring): a critically damped spring, stiffness 500, mass 1 —
       springy off the mark, easing in, stopping dead on target with no overshoot
       and no bounce. Copied rather than promoted to tokens.css because that file
       says tuned curves stay next to the thing they animate.
       No overshoot is the point at this size. A 14px mark that overran its place
       and settled back would read as a wobble, not as juice; the spring's long
       tail is where the smoothness lives.

       THE FALLBACK IS NOT DECORATION. linear() arrived in Safari 17.4 (March
       2024), and on an engine that does not know it the custom property still
       parses — it is a custom property, anything parses — and then poisons every
       `transition` shorthand that references it: invalid at computed-value time
       takes the WHOLE shorthand to its initial value, which is a duration of 0s.
       Not a plainer curve: no animation at all, on that device only. So the
       default here is an ordinary bezier of the same shape and the spring is
       swapped in only where @supports can prove the engine parses it. */
    --dot-spring: cubic-bezier(0.22, 0.9, 0.24, 1);

    /* Travel and fade are timed apart, so the mark is never a ghost drifting
       through the middle of its own journey: it is fully painted well before it
       lands. Arriving takes the longer curve, leaving gets out of the way.
       --dot-out-tail is the longer of the two exit numbers and nothing else —
       the delay on `visibility` below, which has to outlast the slide. */
    --dot-in-move: 0.3s;
    --dot-in-fade: 0.18s;
    --dot-out-move: 0.24s;
    --dot-out-fade: 0.16s;
    --dot-out-tail: 0.24s;

    position: fixed;
    left: 0;
    right: 0;
    bottom: 0.55rem;
    width: var(--chrome-w);
    margin-inline: auto;
    z-index: 200;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: flex-end;
  }

  /* The column's edge is where the ARTICLES pill ends, and what has to meet it
     is the 12px MARK — not the 28px of tap target around it, which would leave
     the squares eight pixels short of the line, reading as almost aligned. So
     the hit areas overhang the column by their own padding. Desktop only: 1025px
     is where chrome.css puts the nav links on screen, and below it there is no
     nav edge to answer to — the row is centred on the page instead. */
  @media (min-width: 1025px) {
    .palette-dot {
      margin-right: calc((var(--dot-mark) - var(--dot-hit)) / 2);
    }
  }

  /* Only where the engine can prove it parses linear() — see --dot-spring
     above. Same physics as chrome.css's --menu-spring. */
  @supports (transition-timing-function: linear(0 0%, 1 100%)) {
    .palette-corner {
      --dot-spring: linear(
        0 0%,
        0.095 6%,
        0.276 11%,
        0.458 17%,
        0.611 22%,
        0.728 28%,
        0.814 33%,
        0.875 39%,
        0.917 44%,
        0.946 50%,
        0.964 56%,
        0.977 61%,
        0.985 67%,
        0.991 72%,
        0.994 78%,
        0.996 83%,
        0.998 89%,
        1 100%
      );
    }
  }

  /* Which slot the off mark is heading for. The applied rules come second and
     out-specify the memory: while a palette is on, the arrangement is read
     straight off <html> — no script involved, so a page that loads with a
     palette already restored paints it in the right place on the first frame.
     [data-family] on the corner is only the MEMORY of the last family used, and
     it only decides where the hidden mark waits, which is why a stale value can
     never show up as a wrong layout. */
  :root[data-palette]:not([data-palette$="-light"]) .palette-corner {
    --off-place: 1;
    --light-place: -1;
  }
  .palette-corner[data-family="dark"] {
    --off-place: 1;
  }
  :root[data-palette] .palette-corner {
    --pair-rest: 0px;
  }

  /* 28px of button around a 12px mark: the mark is the design, the padding is
     the hit target. WCAG 2.5.8 asks for 24px, and a 12px tap target on a phone
     is a coin toss regardless of what the spec says. The hit areas sit flush
     against one another, which is what puts 16px of air between the marks. */
  .palette-dot {
    width: var(--dot-hit);
    height: var(--dot-hit);
    padding: 0;
    border: 0;
    background: none;
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  /* The mark itself, drawn as a pseudo-element rather than as the button's own
     background so that a fill and an empty outline can be the same 12px square —
     a border on the button would outline the 28px hit area instead. All three
     are that one size and hold it: nothing here grows, on hover or otherwise.

     Three bare squares, no hairline around them. A filled mark shows the accent
     of a palette that is NOT on screen, so a dark palette's bright accent can
     land on a light page — 1.72:1 at worst, the number `npm run a11y` prints —
     and the fill is now the whole of the mark, so that is the contrast the
     control has in that one combination. Everywhere else it is 7:1 or better.
     Whoever restores the boundary: it was `outline: 1px solid var(--text-muted)`
     here, and --text-muted is gated at 4.5:1 on every surface of every palette. */
  .palette-dot::before {
    content: "";
    width: var(--dot-mark);
    height: var(--dot-mark);
  }

  /* --palette-dot-light / --palette-dot-dark are set on .palette-corner by
     palette.client.ts. The fallback is the accent in force, so a corner whose
     script has not run yet (or never runs) still shows marks of the right kind
     in a colour that belongs to the page. */
  .palette-dot--light::before {
    background: var(--palette-dot-light, var(--accent));
  }
  .palette-dot--dark::before {
    background: var(--palette-dot-dark, var(--accent));
  }

  /* Off: the same square with its middle taken out, in the accent it is about to
     clear — the one mark whose colour is guaranteed to clear 3:1 on the page it
     sits on, because it IS the page's accent. Box-sizing is border-box site-wide
     (base.css), so the 2px edge is drawn inside the same 12px as the other two
     and all three squares measure the same. */
  .palette-dot--off::before {
    border: 2px solid var(--accent);
  }

  /* ── The off mark comes and goes ─────────────────────────────────────────
     Keyed on <html>, the same attribute every palette in palettes.css is keyed
     on, so there is one source of truth for "a palette is on" and no class for a
     script to forget to remove.

     `visibility`, not `display`. Both take the button out of the tab order — an
     invisible control must never be a tab stop that appears to go nowhere — but
     display cannot be transitioned, and the pair below has to be able to slide
     over while this one arrives. Its slot in the layout is therefore always
     there; what changes is whether anything is painted in it. On the desktop
     stack that costs nothing, because the corner hangs from a fixed bottom edge
     and an empty slot at the top of it moves neither of the marks below.

     The visibility switch itself is instantaneous either way — it is the DELAY
     that matters. Going out, it waits the full --dot-out so the mark stays
     painted through its own slide; the same trick, for the same reason, as
     .card__panel in Carousel.astro. Shorter and it would blink out mid-move;
     longer and it would stay focusable after it had gone. */
  .palette-dot--off {
    visibility: hidden;
    opacity: 0;
    /* Waiting half a slot BELOW the slot it is heading for, so it always rises
       the same short distance into place — whether that place is the top of the
       stack or the gap it has to open between the two squares. The mobile block
       at the foot of this file sends it sideways instead. */
    translate: 0 calc(var(--off-place) * var(--dot-hit) + var(--dot-shift));
  }
  :root[data-palette] .palette-dot--off {
    visibility: visible;
    opacity: 1;
    translate: 0 calc(var(--off-place) * var(--dot-hit));
  }

  /* Pushed clear when the off mark lands between the two squares. Same curve,
     same milliseconds, so the arrival and the shove are one movement. */
  .palette-dot--light {
    translate: 0 calc(var(--light-place) * var(--dot-hit));
  }

  /* The choreography, and the reason all three marks share one declaration:
     they move as one object. On mobile the pair slides right by exactly the
     distance the off mark slides left, on the same curve over the same
     milliseconds, so what you see is a group of three opening out from the
     centre rather than three elements each doing something.

     Gated on [data-ready] like the colour transitions above: on first paint the
     off state — or the on state, if a palette came back from storage — is simply
     how the page renders, with nothing to animate from. */
  .palette-corner[data-ready] .palette-dot {
    transition:
      translate var(--dot-out-move) var(--dot-spring),
      opacity var(--dot-out-fade) linear,
      visibility 0s linear var(--dot-out-tail);
  }
  :root[data-palette] .palette-corner[data-ready] .palette-dot {
    transition:
      translate var(--dot-in-move) var(--dot-spring),
      opacity var(--dot-in-fade) ease-out,
      visibility 0s;
  }

  /* Nothing grows on hover. The marks hold one size, and the only thing that
     answers a keyboard is the global :focus-visible ring in base.css, which
     draws around the 28px button rather than resizing the 12px square inside it.
     (A hover-size change was also a sticky-hover trap on touch, where :hover
     latches after a tap and does not let go until something else is tapped.) */

  /* Only once the script has set the real accents (see palette.client.ts): the
     first assignment is a correction from the fallback above, and a mark fading
     from one colour to another on every page load reads as a bug. After that,
     changes ride the site-wide colour transition, so the corner recolours at the
     same rate as everything it just recoloured.

     The size change is not gated the same way — it can only ever come from a
     hover or a focus, which cannot happen before the script runs. */
  /* Only the fill, and only for the one change it has: a mark that is not the
     applied one repaints when its family draws a new offer, and that is a jump
     from one hex to another with nothing interpolating it. The off mark's border
     needs no entry here — it is var(--accent), and palettes.css crossfades the
     token itself, so a second transition on top would only lag behind it. */
  .palette-corner[data-ready] .palette-dot::before {
    transition: background-color var(--dur-fast) var(--ease-out);
  }

  /* Reduced motion. The durations are zeroed at their source rather than by a
     `transition: none` override, because the rules above are specific enough
     (`:root[data-palette] …`) that an override would have to out-specify them
     and would then be one more selector to keep in step. At 0s the marks simply
     appear where they belong — and the visibility delay, which is written in the
     same variable, collapses with them. */
  /* REDUCED MOTION IS NOT ANSWERED HERE, and that is a decision rather than an
     omission. It used to zero the travel, which on a phone with Reduce Motion
     switched on — a common setting, far more common than on a desktop — left the
     marks jumping between slots while the page crossfaded around them: the same
     control behaving two different ways on two devices, which is what this is
     meant not to do.

     What that setting exists to spare people is large-area movement — parallax,
     full-screen slides, spin. This is three 12px squares travelling fourteen
     pixels (seventeen on a phone) over a quarter of a second, inside a 28px
     corner. It is an order of magnitude below the threshold, and the fade that
     accompanies it is the substitute those same guidelines recommend for motion
     rather than an instance of it.

     Everything else on the site still answers the preference: the smooth
     scrolling in base.css, the portrait's blink, the carousel. This one control
     is the exception, and putting it back is one block:
       @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
         .palette-corner { --dot-in-move: 0s; --dot-out-move: 0s;
                           --dot-out-tail: var(--dot-out-fade); }
       } */

  /* THE HOME PAGE ASKS FIRST. Hidden there until the visitor has pressed one of
     these somewhere else, which is what data-palette-used on <html> records —
     set before first paint by the inline script in Base.astro, so the marks are
     never briefly present on a page that should open as one clean screen.
     `display: none` rather than opacity or visibility: this is not a hidden
     control, it is a control that is not offered, and it should be out of the
     accessibility tree and the tab order entirely. */
  :root:not([data-palette-used]) .palette-corner--home {
    display: none;
  }

  /* ── Phones and tablets: out of the corner, into the footer ──────────────
     1024px is the site's own line between the two chromes (chrome.css: the nav
     links vanish and the hamburger takes over at exactly this width), so the
     marks change place when everything else does rather than at a number of
     their own.

     `position: static` is the whole move — the row goes back into normal flow
     after <main>, which is the end of the document, and `main { flex: 1 }` in
     base.css means a page too short to scroll still pushes it to the bottom of
     the screen instead of leaving it under the last paragraph. The bottom margin
     is what keeps it off the edge of the viewport, where a thumb resting on the
     phone would find it by accident. */
  @media (max-width: 1024px) {
    .palette-corner {
      /* A quarter larger, both numbers: 28 → 35px of hit area, 12 → 15px of
         mark. Because the hit areas sit flush, scaling the hit area is also what
         spaces the marks a quarter further apart — 28 → 35px centre to centre —
         so there is no separate gap to keep in step with the size. */
      --dot-hit: 2.1875rem;
      --dot-mark: 0.9375rem;

      position: static;
      flex-direction: row;
      justify-content: center;
      align-items: center;
      margin-block: var(--space-block);
    }

    /* The home page stays in flow with the rest of them, and only trades its
       margins. It looked at first like the one page that could not: it is locked
       to a single screen (html.is-fullscreen in base.css), so a row after <main>
       reads like something pushed past the bottom edge. It is not — that lock is
       `height: 100svh` on a flex column whose <main> is `flex: 1; min-height: 0`,
       so <main> yields the 35px and the row lands inside the locked screen like
       any other child.

       AND IN FLOW IS THE ONLY PLACE IT IS SAFE. Fixed to the viewport, with a
       `bottom` offset, the marks were positioned against the layout viewport —
       which on a phone extends underneath the browser's own bottom toolbar. On
       every other route that never showed, because those pages scroll and the
       marks sit at the end of the document; on the one page that cannot scroll,
       they had nowhere to go but behind the chrome. 100svh is BY DEFINITION the
       viewport with that chrome showing, so laying them out inside it is what
       puts them on screen — the page's own lock doing the work, rather than a
       second guess at where the bottom of the screen is.

       No top margin: <main> is already absorbing the leftover height, so the
       only gap that has to be stated is the one below. */
    .palette-corner--home {
      margin-block: 0 var(--edge);
    }

    /* THE ROW OPENS OUT FROM ITS CENTRE. A centred row of three that drops to
       two would re-centre and jump sideways every time a palette went on or
       off. Instead the layout is always three slots wide and the marks are
       offset inside it: with no palette applied the pair sits half a slot to the
       left, which puts the two of them dead centre and leaves the off mark's
       slot empty on the right of them; applying one sends the pair right to
       their true slots and the off mark left into its own, and the three are
       centred again.

       So the off mark arrives travelling LEFT, out from behind the pair, while
       the pair travels right — one symmetrical spread rather than an insertion
       that shoves the others along. Clearing plays it backwards. */
    .palette-dot--off {
      translate: calc(var(--off-place) * var(--dot-hit) + var(--dot-shift)) 0;
    }
    :root[data-palette] .palette-dot--off {
      translate: calc(var(--off-place) * var(--dot-hit)) 0;
    }
    .palette-dot--light {
      translate: calc(var(--light-place) * var(--dot-hit) + var(--pair-rest)) 0;
    }
    .palette-dot--dark {
      translate: var(--pair-rest) 0;
    }
  }

  /* Controls that cannot be clicked on paper, printing over whatever is in the
     bottom-right corner of the last page. */
  @media print {
    .palette-corner {
      display: none;
    }
  }
</style>
